WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.520
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:04.660
Last class we treated a voltage source as ideal.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.620
Today we open that model up and ask why a real cell's voltage can fall when it

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.040
powers a load.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:16.220
First, electromotive force, or E M F, is a historical name.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.100
It is not a mechanical force measured in newtons.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.780
It describes energy supplied by the source per unit charge, measured in joules

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.500
per coulomb, which are volts.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:31.040
In a chemical cell, chemical processes maintain charge separation and supply

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.020
electrical energy.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:37.714
Our introductory circuit model replaces that cell with an ideal E M F in

00:00:37.714 --> 00:00:38.100
series

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.480
with an internal resistance, little r.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.120
The external resistor is capital R.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.660
Both belong to the current path, but they represent different places where

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:48.560
energy is transferred.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.120
When the external circuit is open, current is zero in this steady model.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:59.000
There is no I r drop, so voltage across the cell's terminals equals its E M F.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.520
An ideal voltmeter has infinite resistance and draws no current; a practical

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:06.900
high resistance voltmeter approximates that open circuit measurement.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.140
Once a load draws current, the terminal voltage can differ from the E M F.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:16.020
We will assume constant E M F and internal resistance during each problem.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:20.620
Real cells change with temperature, state of charge and operating conditions.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.440
For JEE, begin by sketching the source and little r as separate series

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:25.860
elements.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.620
That simple drawing makes the rest of the calculations much easier.

00:01:29.904 --> 00:01:33.745
Connect a load across the cell, and let conventional current leave its

00:01:33.745 --> 00:01:34.184
positive

00:01:34.184 --> 00:01:34.464
terminal.

00:01:34.944 --> 00:01:37.544
This is discharge: the cell supplies energy.

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:43.515
The E M F must account for the voltage drop inside and outside, so E equals I

00:01:43.515 --> 00:01:43.584
R

00:01:43.584 --> 00:01:45.364
plus I r.

00:01:45.364 --> 00:01:48.944
Collect the terms to get current equal to E divided by R plus r.

00:01:49.824 --> 00:01:54.495
Voltage available at the terminals is E minus I r, and that same voltage

00:01:54.495 --> 00:01:54.884
equals

00:01:54.884 --> 00:01:56.744
I R across the external load.

00:01:57.404 --> 00:02:02.464
Let's use twelve volts of E M F, one ohm of internal resistance and five ohms

00:02:02.464 --> 00:02:03.584
of external resistance.

00:02:04.664 --> 00:02:09.284
Total resistance is six ohms, current is two amperes, and terminal voltage is

00:02:09.284 --> 00:02:09.884
ten volts.

00:02:10.484 --> 00:02:14.524
The missing two volts are the internal drop, not a disappearance of charge.

00:02:15.504 --> 00:02:17.024
Now reduce the load resistance.

00:02:17.864 --> 00:02:22.244
Current rises, which increases the internal drop, so terminal voltage falls in

00:02:22.244 --> 00:02:23.604
this constant parameter model.

00:02:23.604 --> 00:02:28.624
At an open circuit, current approaches zero and terminal voltage approaches

00:02:28.624 --> 00:02:29.264
twelve volts.

00:02:29.964 --> 00:02:33.164
At a model short circuit, external resistance approaches zero.

00:02:33.844 --> 00:02:38.164
If internal resistance is positive, current approaches E over r and terminal

00:02:38.164 --> 00:02:39.324
voltage approaches zero.

00:02:40.044 --> 00:02:43.956
That is a mathematical limit, not a practical experiment to perform: real

00:02:43.956 --> 00:02:44.224
cells

00:02:44.224 --> 00:02:46.184
also have heating and operating limits.

00:02:47.184 --> 00:02:50.664
In numerical work, distinguish the E M F from the load voltage before

00:02:50.664 --> 00:02:51.224
substituting.

00:02:51.224 --> 00:02:55.924
Using twelve volts across the five ohm load here would incorrectly ignore the

00:02:55.924 --> 00:02:56.664
internal resistance.

00:02:57.168 --> 00:02:59.128
Now reverse the energy transfer.

00:03:00.068 --> 00:03:03.988
An external source can drive current into the positive terminal of a

00:03:03.988 --> 00:03:04.668
rechargeable cell.

00:03:05.408 --> 00:03:06.688
We call this charging.

00:03:07.468 --> 00:03:11.237
With I defined as the positive magnitude of that charging current, the

00:03:11.237 --> 00:03:11.668
terminal

00:03:11.668 --> 00:03:15.648
voltage required is E plus I r.

00:03:15.648 --> 00:03:20.188
The source must overcome both the cell's opposing E M F and the internal

00:03:20.188 --> 00:03:21.128
resistive drop.

00:03:21.828 --> 00:03:27.068
For a cell with E M F twelve volts and internal resistance one ohm, a charging

00:03:27.068 --> 00:03:31.788
current of one ampere requires thirteen volts at its terminals in our model.

00:03:32.268 --> 00:03:33.988
Don't memorize minus for a cell forever.

00:03:34.868 --> 00:03:38.668
Draw the current direction and state whether the cell supplies or absorbs

00:03:38.668 --> 00:03:39.108
energy.

00:03:40.148 --> 00:03:43.988
Alternatively, define signed current positive when it leaves the positive

00:03:43.988 --> 00:03:44.288
terminal.

00:03:45.108 --> 00:03:50.488
Then V equals E minus I r handles both cases, because charging current is

00:03:50.488 --> 00:03:51.688
negative in that convention.

00:03:52.188 --> 00:03:57.348
Here is another JEE tool: plot terminal voltage vertically against discharge

00:03:57.348 --> 00:03:58.168
current horizontally.

00:03:59.088 --> 00:04:04.088
The equation is a straight line with vertical intercept E and slope minus r,

00:04:04.868 --> 00:04:06.488
assuming those parameters remain constant.

00:04:07.388 --> 00:04:11.568
Suppose measurements are ten volts at two amperes and eight volts at four

00:04:11.568 --> 00:04:12.468
amperes.

00:04:12.568 --> 00:04:17.148
The slope is minus one volt per ampere, so internal resistance is one ohm.

00:04:18.128 --> 00:04:20.828
Substituting either point gives E M F twelve volts.

00:04:21.548 --> 00:04:23.948
Pause and check the intercept against both readings.

00:04:24.688 --> 00:04:28.208
Two measurements can reveal the hidden source model, not just the terminal

00:04:28.208 --> 00:04:29.268
voltage at one load.

00:04:29.568 --> 00:04:33.908
Let's follow the energy using the same twelve volt cell, one ohm internal

00:04:33.908 --> 00:04:35.748
resistance and five ohm load.

00:04:36.348 --> 00:04:37.748
Current is two amperes.

00:04:38.528 --> 00:04:41.168
Source power is E times I, or twenty four watts.

00:04:42.088 --> 00:04:46.548
Load power is I squared R, giving twenty watts, while internal heating is I

00:04:46.548 --> 00:04:48.008
squared r, giving four watts.

00:04:48.328 --> 00:04:50.388
The two outputs add to the source power.

00:04:50.988 --> 00:04:56.228
The fraction reaching the load is R divided by R plus r, here five sixths, or

00:04:56.228 --> 00:04:57.708
about eighty three point three percent.

00:04:58.288 --> 00:05:02.308
Now ask a different question: which load receives the greatest power for fixed

00:05:02.308 --> 00:05:03.568
E and positive r?

00:05:04.248 --> 00:05:09.008
Substitute current into load power to get E squared R divided by R plus r, all

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:09.248
squared.

00:05:10.048 --> 00:05:14.568
Rewrite the denominator divided by R as R plus two r plus r squared over R.

00:05:15.208 --> 00:05:20.128
The sum R plus r squared over R is at least two r, with equality when R equals

00:05:20.128 --> 00:05:20.488
r.

00:05:21.048 --> 00:05:25.108
Therefore load power is greatest at resistance matching: capital R equals

00:05:25.108 --> 00:05:25.608
little r.

00:05:25.608 --> 00:05:30.728
Our cell then has current six amperes, terminal voltage six volts and load

00:05:30.728 --> 00:05:31.888
power thirty six watts.

00:05:32.668 --> 00:05:36.401
Internal heating is also thirty six watts, so efficiency is only fifty

00:05:36.401 --> 00:05:36.828
percent.

00:05:37.468 --> 00:05:41.128
A very large load resistance gives high efficiency but very small power.

00:05:41.768 --> 00:05:42.688
These are different goals.

00:05:43.268 --> 00:05:47.537
For JEE, identify whether the question asks for greatest current, greatest

00:05:47.537 --> 00:05:47.768
load

00:05:47.768 --> 00:05:49.248
power or greatest efficiency.

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:50.088
The same circuit can give three different limiting answers.

00:05:50.088 --> 00:05:53.808
Finally, let's combine cells while keeping the assumptions visible.

00:05:54.708 --> 00:05:58.528
Put n identical cells in series with their E M F directions aiding.

00:05:59.468 --> 00:06:04.688
Their E M Fs add to n E, and internal resistances add to n r.

00:06:04.688 --> 00:06:09.248
Current through a load R is n E divided by R plus n r.

00:06:09.248 --> 00:06:12.908
Reversing a cell changes its signed E M F contribution, while its internal

00:06:12.908 --> 00:06:14.088
resistance still adds.

00:06:14.788 --> 00:06:18.928
Now put n identical cells in parallel with corresponding polarities joined.

00:06:18.928 --> 00:06:23.388
Their equivalent E M F stays E; it does not multiply by n.

00:06:24.188 --> 00:06:27.328
Their equivalent internal resistance becomes r divided by n.

00:06:28.348 --> 00:06:31.828
Symmetry means they share the delivered current equally in this identical cell

00:06:31.828 --> 00:06:32.128
model.

00:06:33.188 --> 00:06:36.954
Compare two cells, each with E M F one point five volts and internal

00:06:36.954 --> 00:06:37.508
resistance

00:06:37.508 --> 00:06:39.828
half an ohm, supplying a two ohm load.

00:06:40.768 --> 00:06:45.088
Aiding series gives three volts with one ohm internal resistance, so load

00:06:45.088 --> 00:06:47.888
current is one ampere and load voltage is two volts.

00:06:48.588 --> 00:06:53.048
Parallel gives one point five volts with a quarter ohm internal resistance, so

00:06:53.048 --> 00:06:56.895
load current is two thirds of an ampere and load voltage is four thirds of a

00:06:56.895 --> 00:06:57.148
volt.

00:06:57.908 --> 00:07:01.668
Series is better for this particular load; it is not universally better.

00:07:02.468 --> 00:07:06.253
For two identical cells, the currents are equal when external resistance

00:07:06.253 --> 00:07:06.568
equals

00:07:06.568 --> 00:07:07.868
the resistance of one cell.

00:07:08.828 --> 00:07:12.408
Larger loads favor series, and smaller positive loads favor parallel.

00:07:13.068 --> 00:07:17.768
Unequal E M Fs in parallel can drive circulating current, so do not apply the

00:07:17.768 --> 00:07:19.028
identical cell formula there.

00:07:19.028 --> 00:07:19.536
We will use Kirchhoff laws for those broader circuits.
